A||||3||fierospeeder||Ventilation for Garage||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||06-19-2001||11:16 PM||||Im going to insulate my garage by putting up insulation between the rafters.  A friend told me to use rafter vents.  <P>Rafter vents-made of styrofoam that leaves an air space between the plywood and the insulation.  It draws air from the overhang vents to the top of the roof.<P>My garage has a pyramid top, the garage is 22X21 i believe.  Almost square and the garage's roof looks like a pyramid.  <P>I was wondering on how to exit the air that comes into the garage??  I know i can use 2 square vents on the two rafters.  But how do the other rafters are able to be vented??  I cant use a ridge vent unless there are ones designed to go diagnolly up without water coming in?  Im going to replace my roof shingles, so i want to do the vents while im putting the new shingles up.<P>thanks<BR>erik  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||06-19-2001||11:23 PM||||If the rafters are open at the top of the wall, you can add vents to the eaves and put a vent cap at the tip of the roof. If you can mange to leave an inch of space between the roof and insulation, convection will draw cool air from the eave vents and keep the roof temp down.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||fierospeeder||06-20-2001||09:17 PM||||I was wondering if its possible to vent all the rafters.  I know i can do the middle ones using a ridge vent. (its a pyramid roof)<P>Im going to install a lot more eave vents but some of the rafters wont have a place for the fresh air to exit.  Unless just having the rafter vents is enough?||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||06-21-2001||08:18 AM||||Yeah you can vent all the ends of the rafters if you want. Just be sure to use a cover on the eaves/overhangs to keep the birdies and bugs from finding a new home. It will help your roof last longer by keeping it cooler||||reg||14
